--- body ---
## Context

Child of tracking ticket 0252. Model B — binding each worktree to the
session that claimed it, so a sibling's guard refuses to mutate a foreign
worktree — closes incident I2, which model A (0296) cannot: a sibling
mutating a foreign worktree is fully self-consistent under A's rev-parse
check. The author deferred the mechanism choice (2026-07-13); this ticket
is DESIGN ONLY — no implementation until the design is ratified.

Two candidate mechanisms (details in
docs/audit-0252-worktree-ownership-2026-07-12.md and the 2026-07-13
discussion):

1. Piggyback on the existing git lock file
   (`.git/worktrees/<name>/locked`, content `claude agent <name> (pid N)`),
   with the guard checking the lock PID is an ancestor of its own process.
   Zero new state; PID identity fragile across resume/background respawn.
2. session_id claim file written by a PostToolUse hook on EnterWorktree;
   guard compares the claim's session_id to its own (hook JSON carries
   session_id on both sides). Clean identity; new artifact with a
   lifecycle to own (ExitWorktree, worktree switch, stale-claim reaping,
   beat.py awareness).

## Actions

1. Experiment: does a RESUMED session keep its session_id? (Decides
   whether mechanism 2's claims survive resume or need a re-claim path.)
2. Map the write points: EnterWorktree tool sessions vs
   Agent(isolation:"worktree") subagent worktrees vs gaze /tmp worktrees
   (see 0300) — which does each mechanism cover?
3. Specify claim lifecycle: creation, verification (in the 0296 guard),
   handoff on worktree switch, reaping of stale claims.
4. Write the design note under docs/ and present the recommendation to
   the author.

## Verification

- [ ] Resume/session_id experiment result recorded with the method used
- [ ] Both mechanisms evaluated against I2 and against the lifecycle map

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Design note merged; author has ratified a mechanism
- [ ] Implementation sub-ticket(s) filed only after that ratification
